(_jg.chapters = _jg.chapters || []).push(
  { n:1, summary:"After Joshua's death, Israel asks the LORD who shall lead the fight against the Canaanites, and He appoints Judah, who invites Simeon to join him. Together they defeat Adoni-bezek, who admits God has repaid him for maiming seventy kings, and they burn Jerusalem. Caleb offers his daughter Achsah to whoever takes Debir; his younger brother Othniel wins her, and she asks her father for springs of water to add to her south land. Judah takes Gaza, Askelon, and Ekron but cannot drive out the valley dwellers, who fight with iron chariots. Benjamin fails to expel the Jebusites from Jerusalem, while Joseph captures Bethel after a resident betrays the city's entrance. The chapter closes with a catalog of unfinished business: Manasseh, Ephraim, Zebulun, Asher, and Naphtali leave Canaanites in the land, often as forced laborers, and the Amorites squeeze Dan into the mountains.",
    context:"The book's unnamed author, likely writing after Israel had kings, surveys how each tribe fared in claiming its inheritance after Joshua's death.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 1:2", text:"And the LORD said, Judah shall go up: behold, I have delivered the land into his hand." },
      { ref:"Judges 1:19", text:"And the LORD was with Judah; and he drave out the inhabitants of the mountain; but could not drive out the inhabitants of the valley, because they had chariots of iron." },
      { ref:"Judges 1:28", text:"And it came to pass, when Israel was strong, that they put the Canaanites to tribute, and did not utterly drive them out." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Partial obedience leaves room for the very influences that later lead God's people astray.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which tribe did the LORD appoint to lead Israel's first battles after Joshua's death?", choices:["Judah","Benjamin","Ephraim","Dan"], a:0, why:"The LORD answered Israel's inquiry that Judah should go up first, and Judah invited Simeon to fight alongside him." },
      { q:"Why could Judah not drive out the inhabitants of the valley?", choices:["The land was too distant from their cities","The inhabitants had chariots of iron","The LORD forbade any fighting in the valley","They made a peace treaty with the valley peoples"], a:1, why:"Judah drove out the mountaineers because the LORD was with him, but the valley dwellers kept their ground because they fought with iron chariots." },
      { q:"What pattern marks the tribe-by-tribe survey that closes this chapter?", choices:["Every tribe completed its conquest quickly","Most tribes voluntarily gave land back to the Canaanites","Most tribes failed to drive the Canaanites fully out and put them to tribute","All the tribes moved to new territory east of Jordan"], a:2, why:"Verse after verse repeats that this tribe or that did not drive out the inhabitants, and when Israel was strong they put the Canaanites to tribute instead of expelling them." }
    ] },
  { n:2, summary:"An angel of the LORD travels from Gilgal to Bochim and charges Israel with disobedience: they have made leagues with the land's inhabitants instead of throwing down their altars, so those nations will remain as thorns in their sides, and their gods as snares. The people weep and sacrifice at Bochim. The chapter then recalls Joshua's death at one hundred ten and reports that a new generation arose which knew not the LORD nor the works He had done for Israel. They serve Baal and Ashtaroth, and God allows enemies to spoil them until they are greatly distressed. Yet the LORD raises up judges who deliver them, and He is with each judge all his days; when the judge dies, the people corrupt themselves worse than before. God deliberately leaves the remaining nations to prove whether Israel will keep His way.",
    context:"The angel's warning at Bochim and the death of Joshua set up the repeating pattern of the whole book: sin, oppression, crying out, and deliverance.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 2:10", text:"And also all that generation were gathered unto their fathers: and there arose another generation after them, which knew not the LORD, nor yet the works which he had done for Israel." },
      { ref:"Judges 2:16", text:"Nevertheless the LORD raised up judges, which delivered them out of the hand of those that spoiled them." },
      { ref:"Judges 2:18", text:"And when the LORD raised them up judges, then the LORD was with the judge, and delivered them out of the hand of their enemies all the days of the judge: for it repented the LORD because of their groanings by reason of them that oppressed them and vexed them." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Each generation must come to know the LORD for itself; forgetting His works is the first step away from Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What was true of the generation that rose up after Joshua and the elders?", choices:["They knew the boundaries of their tribal lands better than their fathers","They knew not the LORD nor the works He had done for Israel","They kept the law of Moses more faithfully than their fathers","They built the house of God at Shiloh"], a:1, why:"The chapter says another generation arose which knew not the LORD nor yet the works He had done for Israel, and then they served Baal and Ashtaroth." },
      { q:"According to this chapter, what happened each time a judge died?", choices:["The judge's firstborn took his place as judge","Israel kept the peace for another full generation","The surrounding nations made a covenant with Israel","The people returned and corrupted themselves more than their fathers"], a:3, why:"When the judge died they turned back and corrupted themselves worse than before, following other gods, so the cycle kept repeating." },
      { q:"Why did the LORD deliberately leave certain nations in the land?", choices:["To prove Israel, whether they would keep the way of the LORD as their fathers did","Because Israel's army was too small to remove them","Because the nations paid valuable tribute to Israel","So Israel would have trading partners among them"], a:0, why:"The LORD said He left the nations Joshua had not driven out to prove Israel, testing whether they would keep His way." }
    ] },
  { n:3, summary:"This chapter introduces the cycle the rest of the book follows. The LORD left nations in Canaan to test whether Israel would keep His commandments, but Israel dwelt among them, intermarried with them, and served their gods. Oppressed by Chushan-rishathaim of Mesopotamia for eight years, Israel cried to the LORD, who raised up Othniel, Caleb's younger brother; the Spirit of the LORD came upon him, he defeated the king, and the land rested forty years. When Israel did evil again, Eglon of Moab, aided by Ammon and Amalek, seized the city of palm trees for eighteen years. God raised Ehud, a left-handed Benjamite, who delivered the tribute alone and killed the fat king with a hidden two-edged dagger, then rallied Israel to seize the Jordan fords and slay ten thousand Moabites; the land then rested eighty years. Shamgar also delivered Israel, killing six hundred Philistines with an ox goad.",
    context:"Chapter 3 runs the cycle twice in miniature, with Othniel and Ehud each raised up after Israel cried to the LORD under an oppressor.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 3:9", text:"And when the children of Israel cried unto the LORD, the LORD raised up a deliverer to the children of Israel, who delivered them, even Othniel the son of Kenaz, Caleb's younger brother." },
      { ref:"Judges 3:15", text:"But when the children of Israel cried unto the LORD, the LORD raised them up a deliverer, Ehud the son of Gera, a Benjamite, a man lefthanded: and by him the children of Israel sent a present unto Eglon the king of Moab." },
      { ref:"Judges 3:30", text:"So Moab was subdued that day under the hand of Israel. And the land had rest fourscore years." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The LORD hears His people when they cry to Him and sends deliverance through those He chooses.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What weapon did Shamgar use to slay six hundred Philistines?", choices:["A sword taken in battle","A sling with smooth stones","An ox goad","A tent nail and hammer"], a:2, why:"Shamgar the son of Anath slew six hundred Philistines with an ox goad and so delivered Israel." },
      { q:"How did Ehud get to be alone with King Eglon?", choices:["He claimed a secret errand, a message from God, so the king sent everyone out","He disguised himself as a Moabite servant","He bribed the guards with part of the present","He climbed through a window of the summer parlour"], a:0, why:"Ehud said he had a secret errand, then a message from God, for the king; the attendants withdrew, and Ehud was alone with Eglon in the summer parlour." },
      { q:"How long did the land have rest after Ehud's deliverance?", choices:["Forty years","Eighty years","Eighteen years","Ten years"], a:1, why:"Moab was subdued that day, and the land had rest fourscore years — eighty years — after Ehud's victory." }
    ] },
  { n:4, summary:"When Ehud died, Israel did evil again, and the LORD sold them into the hand of Jabin, king of Canaan in Hazor, whose captain Sisera oppressed Israel twenty years with nine hundred chariots of iron. Deborah, a prophetess and the wife of Lapidoth, judged Israel under a palm tree between Ramah and Beth-el. She summoned Barak and delivered the LORD's command: take ten thousand men of Naphtali and Zebulun to mount Tabor, and God would draw Sisera to the river Kishon and deliver him. Barak refused to go without her; Deborah agreed but warned that the honor would go to a woman instead. At Deborah's word Barak attacked, the LORD discomfited Sisera's whole army, and Sisera fled on foot to the tent of Jael, wife of Heber the Kenite. She gave him milk, and while he slept she fastened a tent nail through his temples; Barak found him dead, and Israel destroyed Jabin.",
    context:"With Ehud dead, a Canaanite king arises; the LORD answers this time through a prophetess who judges Israel in mount Ephraim.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 4:4", text:"And Deborah, a prophetess, the wife of Lapidoth, she judged Israel at that time." },
      { ref:"Judges 4:9", text:"And she said, I will surely go with thee: notwithstanding the journey that thou takest shall not be for thine honour; for the LORD shall sell Sisera into the hand of a woman. And Deborah arose, and went with Barak to Kedesh." },
      { ref:"Judges 4:14", text:"And Deborah said unto Barak, Up; for this is the day in which the LORD hath delivered Sisera into thine hand: is not the LORD gone out before thee? So Barak went down from mount Tabor, and ten thousand men after him." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The LORD can give victory through whoever trusts Him enough to act on His word.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where did the children of Israel go up to Deborah for judgment?", choices:["To the gate of Hazor","To the palm tree between Ramah and Beth-el","To the river Kishon","To the top of mount Tabor"], a:1, why:"Deborah dwelt under the palm tree between Ramah and Beth-el in mount Ephraim, and the children of Israel came up to her there for judgment." },
      { q:"What would keep the victory from being for Barak's honor?", choices:["Asking Deborah for payment before the battle","Delaying the attack past the morning","Sparing Sisera's chariots","Requiring Deborah to go with him to the battle"], a:3, why:"Because Barak said he would go only if Deborah came, she told him the journey would not be for his honor, for the LORD would sell Sisera into the hand of a woman." },
      { q:"How did Sisera die?", choices:["He fell on his own sword while fleeing","He died fighting at the river Kishon","Jael fastened a tent nail through his temples while he slept","He was captured and executed at Hazor"], a:2, why:"Sisera fled to Jael's tent, drank milk, and fell asleep; she took a tent nail and a hammer and fastened it through his temples, and Barak found him dead." }
    ] },
  { n:5, summary:"Deborah and Barak sing a victory song on the day the LORD delivered Israel from Sisera. They call on kings and princes to hear praise to the LORD, who went forth from Seir and made the mountains tremble. The song remembers the misery of Shamgar's and Jael's days, when highways were empty and villages undefended, until Deborah arose as a mother in Israel. It praises the volunteers who offered themselves, especially Zebulun and Naphtali, who jeoparded their lives unto the death, while Reuben, Gilead, Dan, and Asher held back. The kings of Canaan fought at Taanach by the waters of Megiddo, but the stars in their courses and the river Kishon fought for Israel. The song blesses Jael, imagines Sisera's mother waiting at her window, and closes by praying that the LORD's enemies perish and those who love Him be as the sun in its might; the land then had rest forty years.",
    context:"The victory song retells chapter 4's battle and names which tribes joined the fight and which stayed home.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 5:2", text:"Praise ye the LORD for the avenging of Israel, when the people willingly offered themselves." },
      { ref:"Judges 5:20", text:"They fought from heaven; the stars in their courses fought against Sisera." },
      { ref:"Judges 5:31", text:"So let all thine enemies perish, O LORD: but let them that love him be as the sun when he goeth forth in his might. And the land had rest forty years." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Victory belongs to the LORD, and His people honor Him most when they offer themselves willingly.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which two tribes does the song praise for jeoparding their lives unto the death?", choices:["Zebulun and Naphtali","Ephraim and Benjamin","Reuben and Gad","Judah and Simeon"], a:0, why:"The song says Zebulun and Naphtali were a people that jeoparded their lives unto the death in the high places of the field." },
      { q:"Whom did the angel of the LORD bitterly curse in the song, and why?", choices:["Sisera, for his twenty years of oppression","Jabin, for his idols","Meroz, because its inhabitants came not to the help of the LORD","Harosheth, for hiding the chariots"], a:2, why:"The angel of the LORD cursed the inhabitants of Meroz bitterly because they came not to the help of the LORD against the mighty." },
      { q:"How does the song's closing blessing describe those who love the LORD?", choices:["They are as the stars in their courses","They are as the sun when he goeth forth in his might","They are like the river Kishon in flood","They are blessed above women in the tent"], a:1, why:"The song ends praying that the LORD's enemies perish but that those who love Him be as the sun when he goeth forth in his might." }
    ] },
  { n:6, summary:"Israel again does evil, and Midian oppresses them seven years: every harvest, swarms of Midianites, Amalekites, and eastern tribes sweep the land like grasshoppers, leaving no sheep, ox, or ass, until Israel hides in dens and caves and finally cries to the LORD. God first sends a prophet reminding them who delivered them from Egypt. Then an angel appears to Gideon, who is threshing wheat by the winepress to hide it, and calls him a mighty man of valour. Gideon protests that his family is poor and he is the least in his father's house, but the LORD promises, Surely I will be with thee. Fire consumes Gideon's offering, and he builds an altar called Jehovah-shalom. That night he tears down his father's altar of Baal; when the city demands his death, Joash answers that Baal should plead for himself. Gideon gathers an army and, with a fleece, twice asks God for signs.",
    context:"Midian's harvest-destroying raids drove Israel to dens and caves; against that fear the LORD called a man from the least place in his father's house.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 6:12", text:"And the angel of the LORD appeared unto him, and said unto him, The LORD is with thee, thou mighty man of valour." },
      { ref:"Judges 6:16", text:"And the LORD said unto him, Surely I will be with thee, and thou shalt smite the Midianites as one man." },
      { ref:"Judges 6:24", text:"Then Gideon built an altar there unto the LORD, and called it Jehovah-shalom: unto this day it is yet in Ophrah of the Abi-ezrites." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's promise of presence outweighs every reason we think we are too small to serve Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What was Gideon doing when the angel of the LORD appeared to him?", choices:["Hunting in the mountains","Praying at the altar in Ophrah","Threshing wheat by the winepress to hide it from the Midianites","Drawing water for his father's flocks"], a:2, why:"Gideon his son threshed wheat by the winepress to hide it from the Midianites when the angel sat under the oak in Ophrah." },
      { q:"Why did Joash say the men of the city should not punish Gideon for breaking Baal's altar?", choices:["Gideon had acted on a prophetic dream","If Baal were a god, he could plead his own cause against Gideon","The altar actually belonged to the Midianites","The city had no authority over Joash's household"], a:1, why:"Joash asked whether the men would plead for Baal, and said if he be a god, let him plead for himself, because one had cast down his altar." },
      { q:"What name did Gideon give the altar he built at Ophrah?", choices:["Jehovah-jireh","Jehovah-nissi","Ebenezer","Jehovah-shalom"], a:3, why:"After the LORD said, Peace be unto thee; fear not, Gideon built an altar there and called it Jehovah-shalom." }
    ] },
  { n:7, summary:"Beside the well of Harod, Gideon's army faces Midian's vast host, but the LORD says the people are too many, lest Israel claim its own hand has saved it. Twenty-two thousand fearful men go home; ten thousand remain. At the water, only three hundred who lap like a dog are chosen, and the rest are sent to their tents. That night the LORD lets Gideon overhear a Midianite soldier telling of a dream: a barley cake tumbled into the camp and overthrew a tent; his friend interprets it as the sword of Gideon. Strengthened, Gideon divides his three hundred into three companies, each with a trumpet, a pitcher, and a lamp. At the middle watch they blow trumpets, break pitchers, and cry, The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon. The enemy host turns its swords against each other and flees; Israel pursues, and Ephraim captures the fords and kills the princes Oreb and Zeeb.",
    context:"Every step of the battle plan — the shrinking army, the dream, the pitchers — is the LORD's, so the glory cannot belong to Israel.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 7:2", text:"And the LORD said unto Gideon, The people that are with thee are too many for me to give the Midianites into their hands, lest Israel vaunt themselves against me, saying, Mine own hand hath saved me." },
      { ref:"Judges 7:7", text:"And the LORD said unto Gideon, By the three hundred men that lapped will I save you, and deliver the Midianites into thine hand: and let all the other people go every man unto his place." },
      { ref:"Judges 7:20", text:"And the three companies blew the trumpets, and brake the pitchers, and held the lamps in their left hands, and the trumpets in their right hands to blow withal: and they cried, The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God may strip away our numbers and strength so that no one can mistake who won the victory.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many men finally stayed with Gideon for the battle?", choices:["Ten thousand","Seven hundred","Three thousand","Three hundred"], a:3, why:"Twenty-two thousand went home for fear, and after the water test only the three hundred men who lapped remained with Gideon." },
      { q:"Why did the LORD insist on reducing Gideon's army?", choices:["Lest Israel vaunt themselves and say, Mine own hand hath saved me","Because the army carried too little food for a long siege","Because many soldiers had been wounded in earlier battles","So the army could move silently by night"], a:0, why:"The LORD said the people were too many, lest Israel claim victory by their own hand instead of crediting Him." },
      { q:"What did each of Gideon's three hundred carry?", choices:["A sword, a shield, and a spear","A trumpet, an empty pitcher, and a lamp within it","A sling and five smooth stones","A ram's horn alone with no light"], a:1, why:"Gideon divided the men into three companies and put a trumpet in every man's hand, with empty pitchers and lamps within the pitchers." }
    ] },
  { n:8, summary:"After the victory, Ephraim angrily asks why Gideon did not call them to fight; his modest answer about gleaning grapes and the princes they captured quiets them. With three hundred faint men Gideon crosses Jordan pursuing Zebah and Zalmunna, the kings of Midian, but Succoth and Penuel refuse bread for his exhausted troops, and he vows consequences. He surprises fifteen thousand men at Karkor, takes the two kings, then punishes Succoth's elders with thorns and breaks Penuel's tower. Learning the kings had killed his brothers at Tabor, he executes them. Israel offers Gideon and his sons the throne, but he answers that the LORD shall rule over them. He asks only the golden earrings of the spoil and makes an ephod, which becomes a snare to him and to all Israel. Midian never rises again, and the land is quiet forty years; yet after Gideon dies, Israel turns again to Baal and forgets his family's kindness.",
    context:"Gideon's last campaign shows both resolve and flaw: the ephod he made drew Israel back into idolatry within his own lifetime.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 8:23", text:"And Gideon said unto them, I will not rule over you, neither shall my son rule over you: the LORD shall rule over you." },
      { ref:"Judges 8:27", text:"And Gideon made an ephod thereof, and put it in his city, even in Ophrah: and all Israel went thither a whoring after it: which thing became a snare unto Gideon, and to his house." },
      { ref:"Judges 8:34", text:"And the children of Israel remembered not the LORD their God, who had delivered them out of the hands of all their enemies on every side:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Refusing a crown is not enough; even a good leader must guard against whatever quietly draws hearts from God.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which two cities refused bread to Gideon's faint army?", choices:["Shechem and Ophrah","Succoth and Penuel","Nobah and Jogbehah","Arumah and Karkor"], a:1, why:"The princes of Succoth and then the men of Penuel both asked whether Zebah and Zalmunna were already in his hand before giving bread." },
      { q:"What became of the golden ephod Gideon made from the spoil?", choices:["It was placed in the tabernacle at Shiloh","Gideon wore it whenever he judged Israel","It became a snare, and Israel went astray after it","It was divided among Gideon's seventy sons"], a:2, why:"Gideon put the ephod in his city Ophrah, and all Israel went astray after it; it became a snare to Gideon and to his house." },
      { q:"When Israel asked Gideon to rule over them, what did he answer?", choices:["He refused, saying the LORD shall rule over you","He accepted on condition of a standing army","He named his son as king in his place","He asked the tribes to vote on it"], a:0, why:"Gideon answered that neither he nor his son would rule, for the LORD shall rule over you." }
    ] },
  { n:9, summary:"Abimelech, Gideon's son by a Shechem concubine, persuades his mother's relatives that one ruler is better than seventy brothers. With seventy pieces of silver from Baal-berith's house he hires reckless men and murders his seventy brothers on one stone; only Jotham, the youngest, escapes. The men of Shechem crown Abimelech king, but Jotham cries from mount Gerizim a fable: the olive, fig, and vine refuse kingship, while the worthless bramble accepts and threatens fire. If the people have dealt truly, let them rejoice; if not, let fire come out from Abimelech and from Shechem and consume them both. Three years later God sends an evil spirit between them; Shechem robs and rebels, championing Gaal. Abimelech destroys the city, slays about a thousand who shelter in the temple stronghold, and burns a tower at Thebez, where a woman's millstone cracks his skull; his armourbearer kills him, and God renders the wickedness back on their heads.",
    context:"Israel's first recorded attempt at a human king comes by murder and bribery, and the chapter shows God judging it.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 9:15", text:"And the bramble said unto the trees, If in truth ye anoint me king over you, then come and put your trust in my shadow: and if not, let fire come out of the bramble, and devour the cedars of Lebanon." },
      { ref:"Judges 9:56", text:"Thus God rendered the wickedness of Abimelech, which he did unto his father, in slaying his seventy brethren:" },
      { ref:"Judges 9:57", text:"And all the evil of the men of Shechem did God render upon their heads: and upon them came the curse of Jotham the son of Jerubbaal." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Power gained by violence invites judgment; God renders wickedness on the heads of those who do it.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which of Gideon's sons escaped Abimelech's massacre of the seventy?", choices:["Gaal","Zebul","Jotham","Jether"], a:2, why:"Jotham the youngest son of Jerubbaal hid himself when his brothers were slain on one stone, and later denounced Abimelech from mount Gerizim." },
      { q:"In Jotham's fable, what did the bramble's answer foreshadow?", choices:["Fire that would come out of the bramble and devour both Abimelech and Shechem","Peace and shade under Abimelech's rule","A harvest as rich as the olive's fatness","Abimelech's submission to the Philistines"], a:0, why:"The bramble threatened fire to devour the cedars, and Jotham applied it: if they dealt falsely, fire would come out from Abimelech and from the men of Shechem to consume each other." },
      { q:"How did Abimelech die at Thebez?", choices:["He fell before Gaal's men at the city gate","The men of Shechem burned the tower with him inside","Jotham's forces captured and executed him","A woman cast a millstone that broke his skull, and his armourbearer slew him"], a:3, why:"A certain woman threw a piece of millstone on Abimelech's head and broke his skull; he had his armourbearer thrust him through so it would not be said a woman slew him." }
    ] },
  { n:10, summary:"After Abimelech, two minor judges bring quiet generations: Tola of Issachar judges twenty-three years, and Jair the Gileadite twenty-two, with thirty sons riding thirty ass colts over thirty cities. Then Israel does evil again, serving the gods of Syria, Zidon, Moab, Ammon, and the Philistines, and the LORD sells them into the hands of the Philistines and Ammonites, who crush Gilead eighteen years and cross Jordan to distress Judah, Benjamin, and Ephraim. When Israel cries out, the LORD answers with a rebuke: He delivered them from Egypt and from every oppressor, yet they forsook Him, so let them cry to the gods they have chosen. Israel confesses, submits to whatever the LORD will do, and puts away the strange gods; His soul is grieved for their misery. The Ammonites encamp in Gilead, and Israel gathers at Mizpeh, promising command to whoever will lead the fight.",
    context:"The cycle reaches a low point: Israel serves the gods of seven neighboring peoples, and at first the LORD refuses their cry.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 10:13", text:"Yet ye have forsaken me, and served other gods: wherefore I will deliver you no more." },
      { ref:"Judges 10:15", text:"And the children of Israel said unto the LORD, We have sinned: do thou unto us whatsoever seemeth good unto thee; deliver us only, we pray thee, this day." },
      { ref:"Judges 10:16", text:"And they put away the strange gods from among them, and served the LORD: and his soul was grieved for the misery of Israel." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The LORD responds to repentance that puts away the false trusts behind the sin, not to words alone.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What marked Jair's time as judge?", choices:["Ten years, with ten daughters in ten cities","Twenty-two years, with thirty sons who rode on thirty ass colts","Forty years, with a great standing army","Eight years, with seven fortified cities"], a:1, why:"Jair the Gileadite judged Israel twenty and two years and had thirty sons who rode on thirty ass colts and had thirty cities called Havoth-jair." },
      { q:"What showed that this time Israel's cry to the LORD was more than words?", choices:["They built a new altar at Mizpeh","They paid tribute to Ammon for relief","They hired soldiers from Judah","They put away the strange gods from among them and served the LORD"], a:3, why:"When the LORD seemed to refuse them, Israel confessed, submitted to His will, and put away the strange gods; then His soul was grieved for their misery." },
      { q:"When Israel first cried out, what did the LORD tell them to do?", choices:["Go and cry unto the gods which ye have chosen","March against Ammon the same day","Wait until the ark came from Shiloh","Send for a deliverer at once"], a:0, why:"Because they had forsaken Him for other gods, the LORD said He would deliver them no more and told them to cry to the gods they had chosen." }
    ] },
  { n:11, summary:"Jephthah, a mighty man of valour driven from home because his mother was not Gilead's wife, flees to Tob until Ammon's invasion makes the elders of Gilead beg him back. He bargains that if the LORD gives victory he shall be their head, and the people confirm it at Mizpeh. Jephthah first sends messengers, arguing from history that Israel took this land not from Ammon but from Sihon king of the Amorites, who attacked them; the LORD gave it to Israel, and three hundred years of possession back that claim. The king of Ammon ignores the argument. The Spirit of the LORD comes on Jephthah, and before battle he vows that whoever meets him on his return shall be the LORD's. He smites twenty cities of Ammon — but his only child, his daughter, comes out to meet him with timbrels and dancing. Heartbroken, he keeps his vow, and Israel's daughters lament her four days each year.",
    context:"An outcast becomes Gilead's head, and a hastily spoken vow before battle brings grief into the deliverer's own house.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 11:27", text:"Wherefore I have not sinned against thee, but thou doest me wrong to war against me: the LORD the Judge be judge this day between the children of Israel and the children of Ammon." },
      { ref:"Judges 11:30", text:"And Jephthah vowed a vow unto the LORD, and said, If thou shalt without fail deliver the children of Ammon into mine hands," },
      { ref:"Judges 11:35", text:"And it came to pass, when he saw her, that he rent his clothes, and said, Alas, my daughter! thou hast brought me very low, and thou art one of them that trouble me: for I have opened my mouth unto the LORD, and I cannot go back." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Words spoken before the LORD should be weighed with care; vows made in haste can bind us to sorrow.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why did Jephthah's half brothers refuse to let him inherit?", choices:["He had joined Ammon's army","He had sold his birthright","He was the son of a strange woman","He refused to worship in Gilead"], a:2, why:"Jephthah was the son of an harlot, and Gilead's wife's sons thrust him out, saying he would not inherit in their father's house." },
      { q:"What was the heart of Jephthah's message to the king of Ammon?", choices:["Israel took the land from Sihon the Amorite when Sihon attacked, and the LORD gave it to Israel","Israel had bought the land with money generations before","The land really belonged to Moab, not to Ammon","Gilead was ready to surrender the disputed cities"], a:0, why:"Jephthah retold how Sihon fought Israel and the LORD delivered his land into Israel's hands, asking why Ammon had never tried to recover it in three hundred years." },
      { q:"When the king of Ammon ignored the negotiation, how did Jephthah respond?", choices:["He surrendered the disputed territory","He retreated across Jordan to Mizpeh","He asked Ephraim to mediate the quarrel","He declared that the LORD the Judge would judge between Israel and Ammon"], a:3, why:"Jephthah said he had not sinned against Ammon, and that the LORD the Judge would be judge that day between the children of Israel and the children of Ammon." }
    ] },
  { n:12, summary:"The Ephraimites, angry that Jephthah fought Ammon without them, threaten to burn his house. Jephthah answers that he did call them and they did not deliver him, so he risked his own life; when the quarrel turns to insults, Gilead crushes Ephraim in battle. The Gileadites hold the Jordan fords and identify fleeing Ephraimites by their pronunciation: asked to say Shibboleth, they say Sibboleth, and forty-two thousand fall. Jephthah judges Israel six years. Three quieter judges follow: Ibzan of Beth-lehem, seven years, who married his thirty sons and thirty daughters into other families; Elon the Zebulonite, ten years; and Abdon the Pirathonite, eight years, whose forty sons and thirty nephews rode on seventy ass colts. These brief notices fill the years between the great deliverers.",
    context:"Ephraim's jealousy, already seen against Gideon, now costs a tribe forty-two thousand men; brief notices follow of three peaceful judges.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 12:4", text:"Then Jephthah gathered together all the men of Gilead, and fought with Ephraim: and the men of Gilead smote Ephraim, because they said, Ye Gileadites are fugitives of Ephraim among the Ephraimites, and among the Manassites." },
      { ref:"Judges 12:6", text:"Then said they unto him, Say now Shibboleth: and he said Sibboleth: for he could not frame to pronounce it right. Then they took him, and slew him at the passages of Jordan: and there fell at that time of the Ephraimites forty and two thousand." },
      { ref:"Judges 12:7", text:"And Jephthah judged Israel six years. Then died Jephthah the Gileadite, and was buried in one of the cities of Gilead." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Pride over who gets credit can wound God's people more deeply than any outside enemy.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What spoken test exposed the fleeing Ephraimites at the Jordan fords?", choices:["Shibboleth","Gilead","Mizpeh","Rimmon"], a:0, why:"The Gileadites told men at the passages to say Shibboleth; the Ephraimites could not frame to pronounce it right and said Sibboleth, and forty-two thousand fell." },
      { q:"Why were the men of Ephraim angry with Jephthah?", choices:["He kept the spoil for Gilead","He fought the children of Ammon without calling them to go with him","He made peace with Ammon instead of fighting","He judged from Mizpeh instead of Shiloh"], a:1, why:"Ephraim demanded to know why Jephthah passed over to fight against the children of Ammon without calling them, and threatened to burn his house upon him." },
      { q:"How many years did Jephthah judge Israel?", choices:["Twenty years","Sixteen years","Six years","Twelve years"], a:2, why:"Jephthah judged Israel six years, then died and was buried in one of the cities of Gilead." }
    ] },
  { n:13, summary:"Israel does evil again and serves the Philistines forty years. In Zorah of the Danites, an angel of the LORD appears to the barren wife of Manoah and promises a son who will be a Nazarite unto God from the womb — no wine, no strong drink, no razor on his head — and who shall begin to deliver Israel out of the Philistines' hand. Manoah prays that the messenger return and teach them what to do with the child. The angel comes again, repeats the mother's instructions, refuses Manoah's meal, and will not tell his name, seeing it is secret. When a kid is offered on the rock, the angel ascends in the altar's flame, and the couple falls on their faces. Manoah fears they will die for seeing God, but his wife reasons that the LORD would not have accepted their offering and announced a son if He meant to destroy them. Samson is born, grows, and the Spirit of the LORD begins to move him.",
    context:"The forty-year Philistine oppression gets its beginning of deliverance through an angel's promise to a childless couple in Zorah.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 13:5", text:"For, lo, thou shalt conceive, and bear a son; and no razor shall come on his head: for the child shall be a Nazarite unto God from the womb: and he shall begin to deliver Israel out of the hand of the Philistines." },
      { ref:"Judges 13:18", text:"And the angel of the LORD said unto him, Why askest thou thus after my name, seeing it is secret?" },
      { ref:"Judges 13:24", text:"And the woman bare a son, and called his name Samson: and the child grew, and the LORD blessed him." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The LORD prepares deliverers before the crisis; a life set apart to Him carries purpose from the beginning.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"To whom did the angel of the LORD first announce Samson's birth?", choices:["To Manoah, her husband","To Manoah's barren wife","To the elders of Zorah","To a priest at the house of God"], a:1, why:"The angel appeared to the woman while she was alone, told her she would conceive and bear a son, and she later ran to tell Manoah." },
      { q:"What did the angel say Samson would do for Israel?", choices:["Destroy every Philistine in the land","Unite the tribes under one king","Rebuild the house of God in Shiloh","Begin to deliver Israel out of the hand of the Philistines"], a:3, why:"The child would be a Nazarite unto God from the womb, and he would begin to deliver Israel from Philistine rule." },
      { q:"Why was Manoah's wife sure they would not die after seeing the angel?", choices:["The LORD would not have received their offerings and shown them such things if He meant them to die","The angel had promised them long life","The correct sacrifice had finally been offered","Only priests die after seeing an angel of God"], a:0, why:"His wife reasoned that if the LORD were pleased to kill them, He would not have accepted the burnt offering and meat offering or told them such things." }
    ] },
  { n:14, summary:"Samson travels to Timnath and demands a Philistine woman in marriage, distressing his parents, though the LORD was behind the choice, seeking an occasion against the Philistines who ruled Israel. On the way a young lion roars at him; the Spirit of the LORD comes mightily and he tears it apart, telling no one. Later he finds bees and honey in the lion's carcase, eats, and shares some with his parents without telling its source. At the wedding feast he challenges thirty companions with a riddle — out of the eater came forth meat, and out of the strong came forth sweetness — wagering thirty sheets and thirty changes of garments. Unable to solve it in three days, the companions threaten to burn Samson's wife and her father's house; she weeps through the feast until he explains, then betrays the answer. On the seventh day Samson answers their words, then, moved by the Spirit, slays thirty men of Ashkelon to pay it, and his wife is given to his companion.",
    context:"The narrator quietly shows the LORD's hand: Samson's marriage quest, though it grieved his parents, became an occasion against the ruling Philistines.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 14:4", text:"But his father and his mother knew not that it was of the LORD, that he sought an occasion against the Philistines: for at that time the Philistines had dominion over Israel." },
      { ref:"Judges 14:6", text:"And the Spirit of the LORD came mightily upon him, and he rent him as he would have rent a kid, and he had nothing in his hand: but he told not his father or his mother what he had done." },
      { ref:"Judges 14:14", text:"And he said unto them, Out of the eater came forth meat, and out of the strong came forth sweetness. And they could not in three days expound the riddle." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Samson's strength lay in the Spirit of the LORD upon him, not in himself.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did Samson find in the carcase of the lion on his return to Timnath?", choices:["A hidden sword","A spring of water","A den of wolves","A swarm of bees and honey"], a:3, why:"He turned aside to see the carcase and found a swarm of bees and honey in it; he took some in his hands and ate." },
      { q:"How did the Philistine companions finally learn the riddle's answer?", choices:["They solved it after three days of study","Samson's wife pressed him until he told her, and she revealed it to them","A servant overheard him speaking in his sleep","His father-in-law guessed it at the wedding feast"], a:1, why:"The companions threatened to burn the wife and her father's house; she wept through the feast until Samson told her, then she told the children of her people." },
      { q:"What does the chapter reveal about Samson's desire for a Philistine wife?", choices:["It was a failure that ended his calling","His parents secretly approved the match","It was of the LORD, who sought an occasion against the ruling Philistines","It proved his Nazarite vow was finished"], a:2, why:"His parents knew not that it was of the LORD, that he sought an occasion against the Philistines, who had dominion over Israel at that time." }
    ] },
  { n:15, summary:"When Samson's father-in-law refuses to let him visit the wife given away to another man, Samson retaliates: he catches three hundred foxes, ties firebrands between their tails, and burns the Philistines' standing grain, vineyards, and olives. The Philistines answer by burning his wife and her father, and Samson strikes them hip and thigh, then dwells at the rock Etam. When Philistine troops camp in Judah, three thousand men of Judah, fearing their rulers, bind Samson with two new cords after he makes them swear not to attack him themselves. At Lehi the Spirit of the LORD mightily comes on him; the cords melt off like burnt flax, and with a fresh jawbone of an ass he slays a thousand men. Thirsty unto death, he calls on the LORD, who opens a hollow place at Lehi; he drinks and revives, naming the spring En-hakkore. Samson judges Israel twenty years in the days of the Philistines.",
    context:"Judah's fear of Philistine retaliation runs so deep that its own men bind the very deliverer the LORD has raised.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 15:14", text:"And when he came unto Lehi, the Philistines shouted against him: and the Spirit of the LORD came mightily upon him, and the cords that were upon his arms became as flax that was burnt with fire, and his bands loosed from off his hands." },
      { ref:"Judges 15:18", text:"And he was sore athirst, and called on the LORD, and said, Thou hast given this great deliverance into the hand of thy servant: and now shall I die for thirst, and fall into the hand of the uncircumcised?" },
      { ref:"Judges 15:20", text:"And he judged Israel in the days of the Philistines twenty years." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God can deliver by the weakest means and sustains those He uses when they call on Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What weapon did Samson use to slay a thousand Philistines at Lehi?", choices:["The jawbone of an ass","Two new cords","A burning firebrand","A sword taken at Lehi"], a:0, why:"Samson found a new jawbone of an ass, took it, and with it slew a thousand men." },
      { q:"Why did three thousand men of Judah come to bind Samson?", choices:["They hoped to collect a Philistine reward","They wanted to make Samson their captain","They knew the Philistines were rulers over them and feared his provocations","They believed Samson had stolen their harvest"], a:2, why:"The men of Judah asked Samson whether he did not know the Philistines were rulers over them, and came to deliver him into their hand." },
      { q:"What does Samson's cry for water after the victory show?", choices:["He regretted striking the Philistines","He looked to the LORD who gave the deliverance to preserve his life also","He blamed the men of Judah for his thirst","He decided to hide at En-hakkore forever"], a:1, why:"Samson credited the great deliverance to the LORD and called on Him in his thirst; God clave a hollow place, water came out, and he revived." }
    ] },
  { n:16, summary:"In Gaza, Samson escapes an ambush by carrying the city gates and bar up a hill before Hebron. Then he loves Delilah, and the Philistine lords bribe her with eleven hundred pieces of silver apiece to discover the secret of his strength. Three times she tests false answers — green withs, new ropes, a woven web — and each time Samson breaks free. Pressed daily until his soul is vexed unto death, he tells her all his heart: as a Nazarite, if he is shaven his strength will leave. While he sleeps on her knees, a man shaves his seven locks; when Samson rises to shake himself as before, he does not know that the LORD has departed from him. The Philistines put out his eyes and chain him to grinding in Gaza's prison. Called out to entertain a feast of Dagon, blind Samson prays for strength once more, pulls down the two middle pillars, and dies with more Philistines than he ever killed in life.",
    context:"The Philistine lords each offered Delilah eleven hundred pieces of silver to learn the secret of a strength that was never Samson's own.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 16:17", text:"That he told her all his heart, and said unto her, There hath not come a razor upon mine head; for I have been a Nazarite unto God from my mother's womb: if I be shaven, then my strength will go from me, and I shall become weak, and be like any other man." },
      { ref:"Judges 16:20", text:"And she said, The Philistines be upon thee, Samson. And he awoke out of his sleep, and said, I will go out as at other times before, and shake myself. And he wist not that the LORD was departed from him." },
      { ref:"Judges 16:28", text:"And Samson called unto the LORD, and said, O Lord GOD, remember me, I pray thee, and strengthen me, I pray thee, only this once, O God, that I may be at once avenged of the Philistines for my two eyes." }
    ],
    takeaway:"When the LORD departs, strength departs; yet He hears even the broken cry that turns back to Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What was Samson made to do in the Philistine prison?", choices:["Build the temple of Dagon","Grind in the prison house","Keep the prison gate by night","Train Philistine soldiers"], a:1, why:"The Philistines put out his eyes, bound him with fetters of brass, and he did grind in the prison house." },
      { q:"What was the real reason Samson's strength left him?", choices:["The razor carried a Philistine curse","His hair alone was the secret source of power","The LORD had departed from him","Delilah's tears had weakened his heart"], a:2, why:"When he awoke to shake himself as at other times, he wist not that the LORD was departed from him; the shaved hair was only the outward sign of his broken Nazarite consecration." },
      { q:"What does Samson's final prayer demonstrate?", choices:["That the LORD never hears the prayers of the fallen","That hair growth alone restores strength","That only priests can intercede for power","That even after his fall, Samson called on the LORD and was strengthened once more"], a:3, why:"Blind and chained, Samson called unto the LORD, asking to be remembered and strengthened only this once, and God answered him." }
    ] },
  { n:17, summary:"The book's final chapters step back from battles to show everyday religion in decay. Micah of mount Ephraim confesses to his mother that he took her eleven hundred shekels of silver; she blesses him, dedicates part of the silver, and has a founder make a graven image and a molten image that end up in Micah's house of gods, complete with an ephod, teraphim, and a son consecrated as priest. The refrain appears: in those days there was no king in Israel, and every man did that which was right in his own eyes. A young Levite from Beth-lehem-judah, wandering for a place, is hired by Micah for ten shekels a year, clothing, and food, and is consecrated to serve the idolatrous shrine. Micah concludes that now the LORD will surely do him good, having a Levite for a priest — mixing the LORD's name with a religion of his own making.",
    context:"The book's closing chapters step back from battles to show everyday religion collapsing; here one family builds its own shrine and hires its own priest.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 17:5", text:"And the man Micah had an house of gods, and made an ephod, and teraphim, and consecrated one of his sons, who became his priest." },
      { ref:"Judges 17:6", text:"In those days there was no king in Israel, but every man did that which was right in his own eyes." },
      { ref:"Judges 17:13", text:"Then said Micah, Now know I that the LORD will do me good, seeing I have a Levite to my priest." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Worship shaped to fit our own preferences is not worship of the LORD, however sincerely His name is used.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How much silver did Micah restore to his mother?", choices:["Ten shekels","Two hundred shekels","Eleven hundred shekels","Thirty shekels"], a:2, why:"Micah confessed taking the eleven hundred shekels of silver his mother had cursed over, and he restored it to her." },
      { q:"What pay did Micah offer the young Levite to be his priest?", choices:["Ten shekels of silver by the year, a suit of apparel, and his food","A tenth of every harvest","A house and a field in mount Ephraim","A place at the tabernacle in Shiloh"], a:0, why:"Micah promised the Levite ten shekels of silver by the year, a suit of apparel, and his victuals, and the Levite was content to dwell with him." },
      { q:"What does Micah's confidence that the LORD would now do him good reveal?", choices:["That owning an image guaranteed God's favor","That religion shaped to fit oneself cannot secure God's blessing","That Levites could command blessings at will","That wealth buys peace with God"], a:1, why:"Micah mixed the LORD's name with idols and a hired priest, thinking form could win favor; the chapter's refrain, every man doing right in his own eyes, exposes the error." }
    ] },
  { n:18, summary:"The tribe of Dan, still short of an inheritance, sends five spies who lodge at Micah's house and ask his hired Levite whether their journey will prosper; he answers that the LORD is their way and says, Go in peace. At Laish they find a quiet, secure people far from any ally, and report a land worth taking. Six hundred armed Danites set out, stop at Micah's house, and steal the graven image, the molten image, the ephod, and the teraphim. When the Levite protests, they invite him to serve a whole tribe instead of one man's house, and his heart is glad. Micah's pursuit ends when Dan threatens his life. The Danites smite and burn Laish, rebuild it as Dan, and set up Micah's idols, with the Levite's line serving as priests to the tribe until the captivity — all while the true house of God stood in Shiloh.",
    context:"Dan's migration north, the theft of Micah's idols, and a priest for hire mark tribal religion replacing the worship God appointed at Shiloh.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 18:6", text:"And the priest said unto them, Go in peace: before the LORD is your way wherein ye go." },
      { ref:"Judges 18:27", text:"And they took the things which Micah had made, and the priest which he had, and came unto Laish, unto a people that were at quiet and secure: and they smote them with the edge of the sword, and burnt the city with fire." },
      { ref:"Judges 18:31", text:"And they set them up Micah's graven image, which he made, all the time that the house of God was in Shiloh." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Idols made by hands cannot even protect themselves; only the LORD truly keeps those who worship Him as He commands.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which city did the Danites destroy and rebuild under a new name?", choices:["Laish, rebuilt and renamed Dan","Zorah, renamed Mahaneh-dan","Eshtaol","Kirjath-jearim"], a:0, why:"The Danites smote Laish, built a city on the place, and called it Dan after their father; the name of the city was Laish at the first." },
      { q:"How did the Danites get Micah's Levite to leave with them?", choices:["They threatened to kill him if he stayed","Micah dismissed him for negligence","The elders of Shiloh summoned him away","They offered a larger calling, priest to a tribe and family in Israel instead of one man's house"], a:3, why:"The five spies told the priest to go with them, asking whether it was better to be priest of one man's house or of a tribe, and the priest's heart was glad." },
      { q:"What did the whole episode prove about Micah's gods?", choices:["That Micah's idols were too sacred for others to touch","That gods made by hand cannot even defend themselves","That the tribe of Dan had the LORD's approval","That Shiloh's worship was corrupt"], a:1, why:"Micah cried that they had taken away his gods which he made, and what had he more; idols that could be stolen could not protect their maker." }
    ] },
  { n:19, summary:"In another no-king-in-Israel episode, a Levite living in mount Ephraim brings home his concubine from her father's house in Beth-lehem-judah. Her father delays him four extra days with feasting, and the party leaves too late to reach home. Refusing to lodge in Jebus because it is a foreign city, the Levite presses on to Gibeah of Benjamin, where no one offers shelter until an old Ephraimite takes them in. Wicked men of the city surround the house demanding the guest; the old man refuses, but the Levite sends out his concubine, who is abused through the night and found dead at the door in the morning. Taking her body home, the Levite divides it into twelve pieces and sends them throughout Israel, calling the nation to consider a deed without parallel since Egypt.",
    context:"Framed by the no-king refrain, this crime at a Benjamite town ignites the civil war of the final chapters.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 19:1", text:"And it came to pass in those days, when there was no king in Israel, that there was a certain Levite sojourning on the side of mount Ephraim, who took to him a concubine out of Beth-lehem-judah." },
      { ref:"Judges 19:15", text:"And they turned aside thither, to go in and to lodge in Gibeah: and when he went in, he sat him down in a street of the city: for there was no man that took them into his house to lodging." },
      { ref:"Judges 19:30", text:"And it was so, that all that saw it said, There was no such deed done nor seen from the day that the children of Israel came up out of the land of Egypt unto this day: consider of it, take advice, and speak your minds." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Where everyone sets his own standard of right, cruelty flourishes and the innocent suffer.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Why did the Levite refuse to lodge in Jebus for the night?", choices:["The city gates were already closed","His servant refused to enter","He would not turn aside into a city of strangers, not of the children of Israel","Jebus had no water for the animals"], a:2, why:"When the servant suggested Jebus, the Levite said they would not turn into the city of a stranger that was not of the children of Israel, and pressed on to Gibeah." },
      { q:"How did the Levite make certain the whole nation heard of the crime?", choices:["He brought his case before Phinehas at Shiloh","He wrote to the elders of every tribe","He called an assembly at Mizpeh himself","He divided his concubine's body into twelve pieces and sent them throughout all the coasts of Israel"], a:3, why:"The Levite divided her, together with her bones, into twelve pieces and sent her into all the coasts of Israel, and all who saw it were called to consider and speak their minds." },
      { q:"What was the nation's judgment on the deed done at Gibeah?", choices:["No such deed had been done or seen since Israel came up out of Egypt","It was an ordinary quarrel among kinsmen","The Levite was exaggerating for sympathy","Such things happened yearly in Israel"], a:0, why:"All who saw the pieces said there was no such deed done nor seen from the day the children of Israel came up out of the land of Egypt unto that day." }
    ] },
  { n:20, summary:"Israel gathers as one man at Mizpeh, four hundred thousand swordsmen, and hears the Levite's account. They demand that Benjamin surrender the wicked men of Gibeah, but Benjamin refuses, musters twenty-six thousand men plus seven hundred left-handed slingers, and prepares to fight. Asked counsel, Israel sends Judah first — and loses twenty-two thousand the first day and eighteen thousand the second. Only after weeping, fasting, and offering sacrifices before the ark, with Phinehas standing to inquire of the LORD, do they hear: Go up, for tomorrow I will deliver them into thine hand. The third day an ambush takes Gibeah, a pillar of smoke is the sign, and Benjamin, drawn from the city, is encircled and cut down — twenty-five thousand men — until six hundred flee to the rock Rimmon. Israel then burns every Benjamite city with its inhabitants.",
    context:"A nation gathers to punish Gibeah yet must learn to seek the LORD through tears and fasting before He grants the victory.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 20:11", text:"So all the men of Israel were gathered against the city, knit together as one man." },
      { ref:"Judges 20:13", text:"Now therefore deliver us the men, the children of Belial, which are in Gibeah, that we may put them to death, and put away evil from Israel. But the children of Benjamin would not hearken to the voice of their brethren the children of Israel:" },
      { ref:"Judges 20:26", text:"Then all the children of Israel, and all the people, went up, and came unto the house of God, and wept, and sat there before the LORD, and fasted that day until even, and offered burnt offerings and peace offerings before the LORD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Confronting evil may be costly, but seeking the LORD with humility turns repeated defeat into deliverance.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How many men of Israel drew sword against Benjamin?", choices:["One hundred thousand","Four hundred thousand","Thirty thousand","Seven hundred thousand"], a:1, why:"Four hundred thousand footmen that drew sword presented themselves in the assembly of the people of God at Mizpeh." },
      { q:"Why did Israel prevail on the third day after losing two battles?", choices:["Benjamin's slingers deserted overnight","Israel captured chariots from the enemy","Gibeah's walls suddenly collapsed","After Israel wept, fasted, and inquired before the ark, the LORD promised them the victory"], a:3, why:"Phinehas stood before the ark as Israel inquired; the LORD answered, Go up; for to morrow I will deliver them into thine hand, and the ambush plan succeeded." },
      { q:"Where did the six hundred surviving Benjamites flee?", choices:["To the rock Rimmon","To the wilderness of Judah","To Laish in the far north","Across Jordan to Gilead"], a:0, why:"Six hundred men turned and fled to the wilderness unto the rock Rimmon, and abode there four months." }
    ] },
  { n:21, summary:"Victory brings grief: Israel has sworn not to give daughters to Benjamin, yet one tribe now faces extinction. Weeping before the house of God, they remember the oath of death against any tribe that skipped the assembly at Mizpeh — and Jabesh-gilead never came. Twelve thousand men destroy the city but spare four hundred virgins, who are given as wives to the six hundred Benjamite survivors at rock Rimmon; still there are not enough, and the oath blocks giving more. So the elders point Benjamin to the yearly feast at Shiloh, advising each man to catch a wife from the daughters of Shiloh as they dance, promising to speak for them to any complainants. Benjamin rebuilds its cities, Israel disperses to its inheritances, and the book closes with its sad refrain: in those days there was no king in Israel; every man did that which was right in his own eyes.",
    context:"The book closes with mercy for a nearly extinct tribe arranged around human oaths, ending on the refrain that frames the whole era.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Judges 21:6", text:"And the children of Israel repented them for Benjamin their brother, and said, There is one tribe cut off from Israel this day." },
      { ref:"Judges 21:17", text:"And they said, There must be an inheritance for them that be escaped of Benjamin, that a tribe be not destroyed out of Israel." },
      { ref:"Judges 21:25", text:"In those days there was no king in Israel: every man did that which was right in his own eyes." }
    ],
    takeaway:"No clever human workaround can heal what only returning to the LORD can restore.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which city had failed to come to the assembly at Mizpeh, and what was done to it?", choices:["Jabesh-gilead, destroyed by twelve thousand Israelites","Shiloh, burned at the yearly feast","Gibeah, spared by the elders","Beer-sheba, put under tribute"], a:0, why:"None came from Jabesh-gilead to the camp, so the congregation sent twelve thousand men of the valiantest, who smote the city." },
      { q:"Why could Israel not simply give more of their own daughters to Benjamin?", choices:["The Benjamites refused to intermarry with Israel","There were no daughters left in Israel","The people had sworn a curse on whoever gave a wife to Benjamin","The law of Moses forbade such marriages"], a:2, why:"The men of Israel had sworn in Mizpeh that none of them would give his daughter unto Benjamin to wife, so the elders had to find another way." },
      { q:"What verdict does the book's last verse pass on the whole era?", choices:["Israel had faithfully kept the covenant of the fathers","The judges had secured lasting peace","The Philistines ruled every tribe by then","There was no king in Israel; every man did that which was right in his own eyes"], a:3, why:"The book closes with its framing refrain: in those days there was no king in Israel, and every man did that which was right in his own eyes." }
    ] }
);
